64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, you are right. Option A relies on the very nursing posts that are empty. We cannot build a home visit program on a foundation of absence.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shion, efficacy is vital, but a program that earns nothing cannot survive our reduced allocation. We need on-site revenue to cover the running costs you ignore.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, you confuse survival with worth. The trial proved efficacy, not profit. If we only fund what earns, we abandon the patients who need us most.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Jonathan, I understand the financial pressure. But Option A is impossible with our current nursing gaps. We must look at options that don’t rely on those vacant posts.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Shion, I agree! Option A is invisible to our booking system. We need a program that fits our current slots and doesn’t clog the car park!","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I understand your passion. But Option C is a joint venture. My minutes show such partnerships collapse when neither side truly owns the rota. We must avoid that fragility.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, you are right. Option A is impossible. Option B generates on-site revenue and fits the therapy staffing. It is the only financially viable path.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Jonathan, you mistake revenue for value. Option B is a luxury, not a necessity. The data proves home visits save lives. We must fund efficacy, not just balance the books.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
